## Context

The host project's config file is the primary documentation surface the
agent reads on every session — AIDE-related or not. Every line that lives
between its opening and closing markers is a line every session pays for,
because the agent re-reads the file from the top each time it starts work.
Shipping the full AIDE methodology into that region made the agent pay the
entire doctrine cost on every unrelated task: a bug fix in an unrelated
module, a commit message, a README edit. The cost was invisible per-session
but compounded across every minute of agent time the host project used.

The fix is to treat the config file the same way the rest of AIDE treats
code: the short, high-traffic surface teaches *what exists and where to
look*, and the long, low-traffic surface holds the actual content. The
full methodology already lives in canonical form under this repo's
`docs/`; a sibling helper installs it into the host project as a
progressively-disclosed `.aide/` hub. This helper's job shrinks to
installing the pointer that names that hub and the instruction that
triggers a crawl into it.

The renderer invariants from the trunk (`src/.aide`) — doctrine lives
in `.aide/docs/`, no AIDE doctrine as string literals in this
submodule's source — and the surgical-insert / marker-based
idempotency invariants from the install service (`src/service/install/.aide`)
still govern. This spec narrows the trunk's doctrine rule to "no
canonical content inlined here" — the pointer template itself is
framework plumbing and lives in source — and inherits the install
service's idempotency contract: re-running through either consumer
entry point detects the marker and writes nothing.

## Strategy

**The delivery layer practices the same progressive disclosure it
teaches.** The methodology's own rule is that agents crawl from a short
pointer into deeper material on demand. Shipping a full-body methodology
into the host config file violated that rule at the delivery layer: the
doctrine that tells agents to crawl on demand was itself dumped up front.
The stub shape restores the invariant — the config file gains a pointer,
the agent crawls into the installed hub only when the task touches AIDE,
and non-AIDE sessions skim past a handful of lines instead of several
hundred.

**The stub is framework plumbing and lives inline in source.** The
stub's sentences teach three things: AIDE exists, here is the hub
path, crawl before acting. None of those are canonical doctrine —
they are the routing layer that sends the agent to the doctrine. The
doctrine itself lives in the hub docs the stub points at. Treating
the stub as canonical would force edits to ripple through a second
canonical file for no content benefit; treating it as inline plumbing
keeps the pointer and its placeholder substitution in one place and
lets the hub docs own every line that actually teaches an AIDE rule.
The dividing line is "pointer" (inline) versus "what the pointer
points at" (canonical under `.aide/docs/`).

**Path agreement with the installer is load-bearing.** The stub names the
host-side hub path and the installer writes into that path. They must
agree by construction, not by coincidence — a shared constant, a shared
helper, a shared configuration surface, whatever the architect chooses.
The worst failure mode of this refactor is a stub that points at a path
the installer never created; the agent reads the stub, follows the
pointer, finds nothing, and concludes the tooling is broken. The
architect's plan owns the mechanism; this spec owns the invariant that
the two sides cannot drift.

**Idempotency stays marker-based, upgrades stay explicit.** The marker
check is unchanged: if the opening marker is present in the host config
file, the helper returns `exists` and writes nothing. A re-run does not
detect a stale stub and replace it in place, because a user who has
edited the marker-bounded region for their team's workflow would lose
that edit silently on the next init. Intentional re-sync belongs to a
future explicit update path, not to this helper's happy path. The stub
being short does not change this — if anything, it makes the argument
stronger, because a short stub is easy for a user to customize and
exactly the kind of content a silent upgrade would steamroll.

**The surgical-insert invariant survives shrinking.** The region the
helper owns got smaller, but the rule that everything outside it is
sacred got no weaker. The helper finds the insertion point, writes its
marker-bounded region, and leaves every other byte of the config file
alone. A user with their own CLAUDE.md content above or below the
region sees that content untouched across every init.

## Good examples

A host project runs the install action for the first time in a
fresh Claude Code project whose `CLAUDE.md` is empty — through the
`aide_init` MCP tool or via `npx aidemd-mcp@latest init` in a
terminal. The helper writes a short marker-bounded region at the top
of the file — an opening marker comment, a header naming the
methodology and the host-side doc-hub path, a handful of lines that
say AIDE exists, that the full doc set lives at the hub path, that
the agent should crawl the hub before writing or acting on any
`.aide` file, and that the agent should never guess AIDE rules from
memory. A closing marker comment closes the region. The file is a
handful of lines long instead of several hundred. The whole stub
template — marker, header, pointer prose, and host-side path
placeholder — lives inlined in this helper's source, because the
stub is framework plumbing whose only job is to route the agent into
the canonical hub.

A developer re-runs the install action a second time on the same
project, through either entry point. The helper sees the opening
marker, returns `exists`, writes nothing, and the install summary
reports the methodology step as already-present. The developer also
runs the install action a third time, after manually customizing the
stub's header with their team's name. The customization survives;
the marker is still there; the helper reports `exists`; their edit
is untouched.

A host project's config file has a hundred lines of unrelated agent
instructions already in it when the install action runs for the
first time. The helper writes its marker-bounded region at the
correct insertion point and leaves every one of those hundred lines
byte-identical above and below. The user's content is sacred outside
the region; the helper is a surgical insert.

An agent session opens the host project on an unrelated task — say,
fixing a bug in a service module. The agent reads `CLAUDE.md`, sees
the short stub, learns that AIDE exists and where the hub is, and
consciously chooses not to crawl the hub because the task does not
touch `.aide` files. Total session cost for carrying the AIDE
methodology was a handful of lines, not several hundred. An hour
later, the same agent picks up a different task that does touch
`.aide` files; this time it follows the stub's pointer, reads the
hub, and crawls into whichever canonical doc its task needs. The
stub and the hub work together exactly the way `study-playbook` and
the user's global brain already do.

## Bad examples

A helper whose inlined stub template copies a paragraph verbatim
from `aide-spec.md` "so the agent gets the rule up front". The copy
ages, the canonical doc moves on, and sessions in the host project
read a stale version of the rule before crawling the current one.
Inline plumbing is fine; inline duplication of canonical content is
the regression this spec exists to prevent.

A stub that points at `aide/` while a sibling helper installs the
hub into `.aide/`. The agent reads the stub, follows the pointer,
finds nothing, and either gives up or invents a different location.
The two sides were never tied together by a shared path source, and
the refactor shipped with the mismatch invisible until an agent hit
it in production.

A re-run that detects an older stub in place, composes a fresh one
from the current canonical source, and writes the new version on top
of the old. The user's hand-edit to the stub header is gone without
a prompt or a warning; the next time they read their CLAUDE.md they
find their customization missing and mistrust the tool. The stub is
small enough to edit by hand, which is exactly why silent upgrades
are worse here than they would have been for a long block.

A stub installed above a fifty-line block of the user's unrelated
instructions — and a future refactor that "cleans up the insertion
point" by rewriting the whole config file from scratch, wiping the
fifty lines out. The region got smaller; the surgical-insert
invariant got forgotten; the user trusted the tool and lost work.
